-Java Control Flow Statements-

## 1] if Statement

## What it is

Executes a block of code only when a condition is true.

### When to use

* When you need to check a single condition

###  Syntax

if (condition) {
    // code executes if condition is true
}



## if-else Statement

###  What it is

Executes one block if the condition is true, otherwise another block.

### When to use

* When there are exactly two possible outcomes

###  Syntax

java
if (condition) {
    // true block
} else {
    // false block
}




## if-else-if Ladder

### What it is

Checks multiple conditions one by one.

### When to use

* When you have multiple conditions to evaluate

###  Syntax
if (condition1) {
    // block 1
} else if (condition2) {
    // block 2
} else {
    // default block
}




## Nested if

###  What it is

An if statement inside another if statement.

### When to use

* When a condition depends on another condition

### Syntax

java
if (condition1) {
    if (condition2) {
        // executes if both conditions are true
    }
}




##  switch Statement

### What it is

Selects one block of code from multiple options based on a value.

### When to use

* When comparing a variable with fixed values

### Syntax

java
switch (expression) {
    case value1:
        // code
        break;
    case value2:
        // code
        break;
    default:
        // default code
}




## Important Points

* `break` stops execution of further cases
* `default` executes when no case matches
* Supported types: `int`, `char`, `String`, `enum`



## Fall-through Concept

If `break` is not used, execution continues into the next case.

switch (num) {
    case 1:
        System.out.println("One");
    case 2:
        System.out.println("Two");
    case 3:
        System.out.println("Three");
}

